  <h3>Initial street survey</h3>
  <p>Going into the Sto-Rox community, I needed to find out what people's day-to-day was like and learn about the diverse lives of the community members. However, I did not yet know the suitable topics to start from or the appropriate language to use. So, I sought the topics and language for the workshops through a simple street survey.</p>
  <p>I was inspired by Professor Dan Lockton's New Metaphors in designing the survey (Lockton et al., 2019). What kind of metaphors do people use to describe the concepts of participatory capital, such as 'neighborhood' or 'finance'? Asking for metaphors for relevant concepts gave me a glimpse into what kinds of topics are on people's minds, as well as the language and sentiments associated with those topics.</p>

  <p><strong>Overall takeaways</strong></p>
  <!-- Image floats left so text wraps beneath it instead of leaving empty space -->
  <div class="not-prose flow-root mt-4 text-base text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
    <div class="mb-6 tablet:float-left tablet:w-1/2 tablet:mr-8 tablet:mb-4">
      <AssetImage dir={dir} name="Co-design-Community quotes" alt="Quotes from the community, such as 'Prosperity as time. It doesn't always mean money.'" lightbox />
    </div>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p><strong class="text-gray-800">There is a general sense of 'it used to be better.'</strong> Sto-Rox hit peak population during the 1930s steel era but has been in decline in population and income ever since. Some people talked about having no hope for the future, while others spoke about the cause of the problem, such as a lack of unity.</p>
      <p><strong class="text-gray-800">There is a sense of community, but it exists in small, disconnected pockets.</strong> Many of the Sto-Rox community members are people who have lived in the area all their lives. They care about the community deeply. However, I observed that people have small, close-knit groups but mistrust the community as a whole.</p>
      <p><strong class="text-gray-800">We need both external and internal motives for community revitalization.</strong> This was an interesting point to me as someone coming from the outside with outside resources. One of the participants said, 'You need guidance but you also need willingness (from the community).' Sensing the value of independence, I want to further investigate the right balance of external and internal motives.</p>
      <p><strong class="text-gray-800">Money is more than money.</strong> It means more time, it implies enablement, it means bigger possibility. Money is a tool. I realized that when talking about finance and capital investment, we need to talk about the core purpose beyond monetary support.</p>
      <p><strong class="text-gray-800">Bringing in diverse racial backgrounds, past trauma, and lifestyles to our design is essential to build a community.</strong> Sto-Rox is very diverse in culture across many spectrums. It is also a neighborhood going through a transition and possible gentrification. How do we keep diversity and authenticity while bringing people together as a community?</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Recruiting</h3>
  <div class="not-prose flow-root mt-4 text-base text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
    <div class="mb-6 tablet:float-right tablet:w-1/2 tablet:ml-8 tablet:mb-4">
      <AssetImage dir={dir} name="Co-design-Recruitingdiagram" alt="Step 1: identify knowledge types. Step 2: match knowledge with collaboration types on a dominant–marginalized, people–task oriented matrix" lightbox />
    </div>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p>I wanted to push the definition of diversity to include class, gender, background, occupation, and world views when recruiting workshop participants. We did this through the creative adaptation of a conventional design tool: the persona. First, I identified the different types of knowledge that I saw in the generative research phase. I tried to balance the existing hierarchy among the knowledge types with collaboration types using a 2x2 matrix. One axis ran from dominant knowledge to marginalized knowledge (which comes from lived experience), and the other ran from people-oriented to task-oriented collaboration styles. Using the personas as a reference, community partners nominated community members who would bring diverse voices to the table. This process preemptively evened out the power differences between the workshop participants.</p>
      <p>Read more about the <a href="https://medium.com/design-master-thesis-journal/who-is-our-co-designer-99c56dd15e58" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="font-semibold underline hover:text-gray-900">final personas here ↗</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Workshop design</h3>
